Title: THE CALL OF THE WILD
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from the novel by Jack London
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S PLACE - DAY

Sunlight filters through tall poplars onto wide lawns and a sprawling house. BUCK, a powerful 140-pound St. Bernard-Scotch shepherd mix with glossy coat and royal bearing, plunges into a cement swimming tank, then bounds after the Judge's sons across green pastures.

(SOUND: distant laughter of children, water splashing, birds calling from the orchards)

NARRATOR
Buck ruled over this great demesne. He was neither house-dog nor kennel-dog. The whole realm was his. Among the terriers he stalked imperiously. He was king -- king over all creeping, crawling, flying things of Judge Miller's place, humans included.

A shadowy figure, MANUEL the gardener's helper, approaches with a rope. Buck follows trustingly into the orchard.

(SOUND: gravel crunching underfoot, rope uncoiling with a faint hiss)

MANUEL
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

The rope doubles around Buck's neck. A stranger takes the ends. Buck growls.

STRANGER
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

The rope tightens. Buck springs, but the stranger grapples and throws him. Buck's eyes glaze as he is dragged to a flag station.

(SOUND: rope creaking taut, Buck's strangled snarls fading to gasps, distant train whistle)

INT. BAGGAGE CAR - NIGHT

The car jolts along. Buck's tongue hurts. He opens blood-shot eyes in rage and bites the stranger's hand. The man clubs him senseless again.

(SOUND: locomotive shriek at a crossing, wheels clacking rhythmically on rails, Buck's furious growls cut short by a meaty thud)

NARRATOR
For two days and nights Buck neither ate nor drank. His eyes turned blood-shot. He was metamorphosed into a raging fiend.

EXT. SMALL HIGH-WALLED BACK YARD - SEATTLE - DAY

Four men carry the crate into the yard. A stout MAN IN THE RED SWEATER signs for it, then pries the crate with a hatchet. Buck explodes out, a red-eyed devil, charging with 140 pounds of fury.

(SOUND: wood splintering under hatchet blows, Buck's savage snarls echoing off walls, crowd on the wall murmuring)

The man shifts a club. Buck leaps -- and is smashed down. Again and again he charges. The club breaks every rush. Blood flows from nose, mouth, ears. A final shrewd blow on the nose crumples him.

(SOUND: repeated heavy club impacts like dull thunder, Buck's roars turning to whimpers, then silence broken only by panting)

MAN IN THE RED SWEATER
'Answers to the name of Buck. Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction, and the best thing we can do is to let it go at that. You've learned your place, and I know mine. Be a good dog and all'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you. Understand?

He pats the pounded head. Buck drinks eagerly, then bolts raw meat from the hand. His eyes show he is beaten but not broken.

(SOUND: water lapped greedily, meat tearing between teeth, low tense drone of wind through the walls)

NARRATOR
That club was a revelation. It was his introduction to the reign of primitive law. A man with a club was a lawgiver, a master to be obeyed.

EXT. DYE A BEACH - DAY

The Narwhal has landed. Buck and CURLY, a good-natured Newfoundland, are turned over to PERRAULT and FRANCOIS. Snow falls. Buck's feet sink into the white mush. He snorts, licks it. It bites like fire.

(SOUND: propeller throb dying away, excited voices of men and dogs, snow crunching under paws)

NARRATOR
Buck's first day on the Dyea beach was like a nightmare. He had been jerked from the heart of civilization into the heart of things primordial. These dogs and men were savages who knew no law but the law of club and fang.

A husky the size of a wolf leaps at Curly without warning. Teeth clip. Thirty huskies circle silently, licking chops. Curly is tumbled, buried under a mass of bodies, screaming.

(SOUND: metallic snap of teeth, sudden frenzied yelps and snarls building to a savage chorus, Curly's agonized screams cut short)

SPITZ, a treacherous snow-white dog, laughs with a scarlet tongue. Francois swings an axe, scatters the huskies. Curly lies torn to pieces in bloody snow.

(SOUND: axe swings whistling through air, clubs thudding on hides, final wet ripping sounds fading under Francois's curses)

NARRATOR
So that was the way. No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you. From that moment Buck hated Spitz with a bitter and deathless hatred.

FRANCOIS harnesses Buck. He hauls a sled, learns "mush" and "ho." Dave nips his flanks. Spitz growls reproof. Other dogs arrive: BILLEE the good-natured, JOE the sour, SOL-LEKS the one-eyed angry one.

(SOUND: harness bells jingling, whip cracks like pistol shots, dogs' paws padding in snow, low growls of instruction)

NARRATOR
Buck learned easily. T'ree vair' good dogs. Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

The team strains under heavy loads. Perrault packs trail ahead. They cross glaciers, the Chilcoot Divide. Dave and Sol-leks transform into eager wheel-dogs. Buck is placed between them for instruction. They nip him into place.

(SOUND: sled runners hissing over packed snow, men's labored breathing, whip snaps urging them on, distant ice cracking on the river)

NARRATOR
The toil of the traces seemed the supreme expression of their being. Buck swiftly lost the fastidiousness of his old life. He learned to steal, to adjust to the law of club and fang. His muscles became hard as iron. Instincts long dead became alive again.

At night Buck digs a hole in the snow. He howls at the stars, long and wolflike.

(SOUND: wind moaning across the divide, faint howling chorus rising from other dogs, Buck's own howl joining in deep vibration)

NARRATOR
He was sounding the deeps of his nature, going back into the womb of Time.

EXT. CAMP ON THE SHORE OF LAKE LE BARGE - NIGHT

Driving snow and cutting wind. The team nests in snow. Spitz bullies. Buck's nest is taken. He springs on Spitz in fury. They circle, but starving huskies invade, crazed for food. They devour the grub. Curly's fate repeats in the chaos -- dogs ripped, blood spraying.

(SOUND: wind howling like a knife, sudden pandemonium of snarls and yelps, clubs cracking on ribs, desperate screams of the attacked)

NARRATOR
The dominant primordial beast was strong in Buck. In the bitter hatred between him and Spitz he betrayed no impatience.

The fight is delayed. They reach Dawson. Discipline breaks. Buck interferes with Spitz's punishments craftily. One night a snowshoe rabbit runs. The pack gives chase under moonlight. Spitz cuts across, kills it. Buck collides shoulder to shoulder. They fight to the death, circling in the snow. The wolfish circle watches.

(SOUND: paws drumming on snow in full cry, rabbit's shriek as back breaks, then only heavy breathing and soft growls as they circle, breaths drifting visibly)

Buck uses imagination -- feints for the throat, sweeps low, breaks Spitz's forelegs one by one. Spitz goes down. The circle closes. Buck stands dominant.

(SOUND: crunch of breaking bone, Spitz's final snarls turning to gurgles, pack's expectant whines rising then fading as they feed)

NARRATOR
Buck stood and looked on, the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast who had made his kill and found it good.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Francois harnesses Sol-leks as leader. Buck displaces him. Clubs fly. Buck dodges, demands the place. He earns it. The team leaps as one. They make record time to Skaguay. But they are sold to HAL, CHARLES and MERCEDES -- slipshod, overloaded, arguing constantly.

(SOUND: harness bells now discordant, arguments overlapping in rising voices, sled creaking under impossible load)

NARRATOR
They were manifestly out of place. The insidious revolt led by Buck had destroyed the solidarity of the team. They went short on dog-food. The dogs starved.

The team staggers, footsore. Billee falls. Hal clubs him dead. Mercedes rides the sled, weeping. They reach White River.

(SOUND: weak paw falls jarring like dull impacts, club striking flesh with sickening thuds, Mercedes's broken sobs)

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP AT MOUTH OF WHITE RIVER - DAY

The sled breaks through rotten ice. Hal, Charles, Mercedes vanish screaming into the hole. Buck, near death, is cut free by JOHN THORNTON. Thornton kneels, searches his starved body.

(SOUND: ice cracking like thunder, screams swallowed by water, then only the river's roar and Thornton's gentle murmurs)

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand. They watch the yawning hole.

(SOUND: river current gurgling over the break, Buck's faint whimper of relief, wind sighing through spruce)

NARRATOR
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time. This man had saved his life. He was the ideal master.

EXT. ELDORADO SALOON - DAWSON - DAY

A wager. A sled loaded with a thousand pounds of flour. Men bank around it. Thornton whispers to Buck.

(SOUND: crowd murmurs building to excited bets, snow creaking under the heavy runners, Buck's eager whine)

JOHN THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck tightens traces, leaps forward on command. The sled breaks out, moves steadily. The crowd roars.

(SOUND: crisp crackling of ice under runners, Buck's claws scarring snow in rhythmic scratches, cheers exploding like a wave)

NARRATOR
Buck earned sixteen hundred dollars in five minutes. He made it possible for Thornton to journey into the East after a fabled lost mine.

EXT. EASTERN WILDERNESS - CAMP - NIGHT

Thornton, Hans and Pete pan gold. Buck lies by the fire, dreaming of the hairy man. The call sounds deep in the forest. Buck wanders, then returns to Thornton in frenzy of affection.

(SOUND: fire crackling, distant wolf howls rising and falling, Buck's playful growls mixed with loving whines)

NARRATOR
He was a thing of the wild, come in from the wild to sit by John Thornton's fire. But the call was sounding, luring him.

The Yeehats attack. Arrows fly. Thornton is killed. Buck descends like a hurricane, ripping throats, scattering them in terror.

(SOUND: arrows whistling and thudding into flesh, Yeehats' panicked screams, Buck's roar like a lion's, blood spraying wetly)

NARRATOR
He had killed man, the noblest game of all. The last tie was broken.

Buck stands over Thornton's body, then turns to the forest. He joins the wolves, running at their head, howling the song of the pack under the aurora.

(SOUND: mournful wolf chorus swelling, paws drumming on snow in unified rhythm, aurora crackling faintly overhead like distant electricity)

NARRATOR
He was the dominant primordial beast. And here may well end the story of Buck. He runs at the head of the pack through the pale moonlight, his great throat a-bellow as he sings a song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

FADE OUT.

THE END.
